my name's james from mountain rock  cleaning and today i'm going to be  cleaning a rug infested with earthworms  the rug was just completely saturated in  mud soaking wet and then on further  inspection i could see what looked to be  little maggots inside the rug turns out  it was probably earthworm larvae it was  a rug that somebody didn't want anymore  so it was just discarded in their garden  basically i just wanted to see if i  could clean it test my skills  essentially and and see you know what i  could do with it  essentially i'm just rinsing the rug  with water so it's just a high-powered  hose and it's just pushing water out  onto the rug to just rinse out as much  of the of the mud as possible  it's not a pressure washer pressure  washer has a lot of power but it doesn't  necessarily shoot out a high volume of  water onto the rug and essentially what  you want to do in the beginning is just  get the water on there as quick as  possible i have a pump attached to the  hose  just so it gives enough pressure and  power so it's not like trickling out  really slowly it's powerful enough that  you can  sort of blast the top of the the rug and  it will just sort of wash away any sort  of um surface soils from the top of the  rug  you're going to be using brushes on the  rug and the water alongside with the  shampoo lubricates the rug to  prevent any kind of damage from from the  machines that you're going to be using  on it all sorts of leaves and twigs and  soil and things are just blasting off  the top of the rug as well as the  earthworms that were in there i wanted  to make sure that i got those washed off  the top of the rug because i didn't want  to turn them all up into the rod so i  wanted to make sure that i got those all  off first before i took any more steps  i'm using just a watering can with a  mixture of  shampoo and  oxygen booster in it and then i'm just  using that to just get the shampoo  distributed over the top of the rug  so that's the rotary machine it has a  rotating brush on the bottom of it and  that is used to essentially get the  shampoo mixed into the rug  so it's easier to then wash out i'm  using a special fibre shampoo it's quite  a mild detergent  pet safe wall safe it's not very strong  it's just enough to break down the soils  in the rug and make it easier for you to  sort of wash it all out  the oxygen booster just helps break down  the soils a lot quicker it can make  things whiter it can bring the colors  out reduce odor and yeah just  essentially make it easier for you to  remove the stains  i wouldn't use bleach at all bleach in  general damages the fibers especially  because this was a wool rug it can  damage the colors in the rug so it's  better to just avoid using the bleach  it's cold water you don't want to use  hot water on rugs because  they tend to have a  problem with shrinking so  you just use cold water and that just  eliminates any kind of potential  problems that you might have with  cleaning them  [Music]  you then use what's called a squeegee  essentially like what you use to clean  your windows and you you scrape off all  the dirt and foam from the top of the  rug  the next step is flipping the rug over  and then cleaning the wash floor  underneath because a lot of the dirt and  stuff is still underneath the rug so i  think it's a good idea to just wash all  that away before you then put the rug  back on top that you've just cleaned  and then again it's pretty much exactly  the same process as doing the front just  start off with getting the rug saturated  with water adding the shampoo and an  oxygen booster again and then just  cleaning back of it with the rotary to  get as many of the stains out as you  possibly can  always clean the back in the front  because when you clean the front it  pushes a lot of the dirt through into  the back  and then also on the flip side when you  clean the bat it then forces a lot of  the  dirt and stuff which is deep in the rug  out from the opposite side if you see  what i mean  [Music]  i usually then use a crb machine on on  the top of the rug it looks a bit like a  vacuum it's like a gray box  it stands for contra rotating brush head  inside it's got two brushes that revolve  in opposite directions and then as they  roll up they they sort of pull the  fibers of the rug up into the machine so  it kind of gives you a much deeper clean  but it also lifts the pile of the rug  making it a bit more fluffy  i'm adding more products to the rug to  sort of break down more soils that are  still left inside you do it until the  water comes out as clear as possible  you'll never get a rug 100 clean there  will always be some dirt inside a rug  you could be using  thousands and thousands of liters of  water unnecessarily for very very little  gain  so there's a point at which it's still  getting little bits out but it's just  not worth wasting so much water in order  to get it out  at that point i'm adding acidic rinse to  the rug it neutralizes all the  chemicals that you've previously used  and it helps to wash them all out it  also helps prevent cellulosic browning  which is a process at which the natural  fibers in the rug can can turn  brown that is called a gandy groom it's  used to remove the pattern left in the  rug but it's also to set the fibres to  help it dry quicker  i'm vacuuming out as much moisture as i  possibly can so that's basically a wet  vac and it's sucking out the moisture  and then it's going into a tank  it's quite an important process because  it is obviously removing a lot of the  moisture from the rug then i put it in a  centrifuge which gets about 95 of the  water out of the rug you hang the rug in  a  warm room with fans and a dehumidifier  and then that just gets the last  remaining bits of the moisture out  i mean don't leave your rug in the  garden  for a start  um but yeah the biggest tip i've got for  taking care of your rug is is lots of  vacuuming the worst thing that d